# The pinned upstreams, one per line: name url commit version kind
#
# Read by deps/fetch.sh and by tools/verify.sh. For anything registered as a
# submodule the commit is also what the parent repository records as the
# gitlink, so `git submodule status` and this file must agree - verify.sh
# checks that they do, because a submodule silently advanced by a
# `git submodule update --remote` is a different measurement under the same
# name.
#
# kind:
#   tool      our own code, full checkout, every demo needs it
#   sdk       a vendor package, sparse checkout of only what a victim compiles
#   mirror    NOT the vendor's own git - see the X-CUBE-SBSFU note below
#   optional  fetched only when named by hand; no demo needs it to run
#   subtree   upstream's files are COMMITTED IN THIS REPOSITORY, so there is
#             nothing to fetch. The commit below is what they were taken from,
#             and it is a record rather than something tools/verify.sh can
#             check - a subtree has no HEAD of its own. Temporary: it goes
#             back to being a submodule when its upstream is public.
#
# Why each one is what it is, which is not a detail:
#
#   flashwhisperer   the analysis half: the scoring rule, the row geometry, the
#                    AES reference. Every recovery here goes through it.
#                    VENDORED AS A SUBTREE while its upstream is private: a
#                    gitlink to a private repository hands a recipient an empty
#                    directory and hands CI an authentication failure, and both
#                    look like something else. It becomes a submodule again on
#                    the day the upstream goes public - deps/README.md has the
#                    two commands, and it is one commit either way.
#   ethtimer         the Ethernet instrument, firmware and host in one project.
#                    It IS the attacker board for the four wired demos, which
#                    is why this repo ships no attacker firmware and no
#                    protocol host code for them.
#   crypto-eval      the ON-CHIP floor for a part a demo uses: same cipher,
#                    same silicon, the victim's own cycle counter instead of a
#                    transport. Nothing here imports it, so it is optional -
#                    but when a demo needs "what would this cost with a perfect
#                    oracle?", the number comes from there rather than from a
#                    second rig built here.
#   stm32cube-f4     the F429 victims compile the lwIP, mbedTLS and HAL THIS
#                    PACKAGE SHIPS, not upstream, because "as the vendor ships
#                    it" is the whole claim. Do NOT add an upstream Mbed-TLS
#                    pin beside it - that is crypto-eval's job, not this one's.
#                    The Drivers are submodules OF the package, so fetch.sh has
#                    to init the ones a victim needs; a sparse checkout alone
#                    leaves empty directories and a build that fails on a
#                    missing stm32f4xx_hal.h.
#   stm32cube-l1     the L152 SBSFU port needs this for the HAL only, because
#                    X-CUBE-SBSFU ships no STM32L1 project. The package version
#                    and the HAL submodule's own version are DIFFERENT NUMBERS
#                    (1.10.6 against 1.4.6); assert each against the file that
#                    states it, or the check warns on every run and means
#                    nothing.
#   uoscore-uedhoc   upstream of the Zephyr module, and where the OSCORE
#                    victim's AES comes from: TinyCrypt and zcbor live in its
#                    externals/.
#   x-cube-subg2     ST DOES publish this one, which is why the 802.15.4 demo
#                    is a clone-patch-build script and not a tarball.
#   riot             the FIDO2 victim is RIOT's own tests/sys/fido2_ctap_hid,
#                    unmodified. The line numbers that demo quotes are against
#                    this commit, and its Makefile warns if the checkout moved.
#   sbsfu            X-CUBE-SBSFU, and the one entry that is not a vendor repo.
#                    There is no STMicroelectronics/x-cube-sbsfu (404, checked
#                    2026-09-26) and ST ships it as a zip, so this is a
#                    third-party mirror pinned to a commit with the release
#                    version asserted out of its own Release_Notes.html. A
#                    mirror can be force-pushed or deleted; a commit SHA cannot
#                    change under you, and the assertion fails loudly if the
#                    tree is not the one the ports were written against.
#
# NOT here, deliberately:
#
#   STM32CubeF7. The F746 attacker firmware is ethtimer's now, and ethtimer
#   fetches its own ST sources with tools/fetch_sdk.sh f746.
#
#   Harmony 3. It is not a clone: MPLAB X's Content Manager installs it, one
#   versioned component at a time (csp, core, net, crypto, net_apps_pic32mz),
#   and the toolchain matters as much as the source - MPLAB X v6.20 exactly,
#   because v6.25 dropped support for that board's Gen-3 PKOB debugger. The
#   component versions get recorded with the Harmony demo when it lands, beside
#   its patches, rather than invented here.
